97412109375, 0.008941650390625, 0.00685882568359375, 0.01091766357421875, 0.019775390625, 0.02618408203125, 0.0177459716796875, -0.023223876953125, -0.0262451171875, 0.01050567626953125, 0.0151824951171875, 0.0163116455078125, -0.0010042190551757812, 0.0005812644958496094, -0.0246734619140625, 0.00689697265625, -0.01021575927734375, 0.0194854736328125, 0.00701904296875, 0.00778961181640625, -0.005184173583984375, -0.0003268718719482422, -0.0233917236328125, -0.00824737548828125, 0.0003917217254638672, 0.01800537109375, 0.025909423828125, 0.0192108154296875, -0.020050048828125, 0.003147125244140625, 0.0285797119140625, 0.0032596588134765625, 0.05706787109375, -0.042572021484375, 0.0198822021484375, 0.0212860107421875, -0.03240966796875, 0.01080322265625, -0.00838470458984375, -0.0007939338684082031, 0.0156097412109375, -0.03228759765625, 0.00975799560546875, -0.005306243896484375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks are essential for achieving low-carbon heat supplies, but their efficient operation is challenged by the integration of heterogeneous and time-varying renewable sources, the need for thermal storage, and increasingly complex, meshed network topologies. Existing optimization approaches often struggle with the nonlinearities and combinatorial complexity inherent in these systems, particularly when considering multiple time steps and realistic operational constraints. This paper addresses these challenges by investigating the global optimization of district heating network operation over multiple time steps, using a stationary nonlinear network model that captures key physical and operational characteristics, such as nonlinear heat losses and flow-temperature coupling, while assuming steady-state conditions within each time step. The model is applicable to networks with cycles and multiple suppliers, but does not account for transient dynamics within time steps.\n\nTo improve the computational performance of a spatial branch-and-bound algorithm for single time-step optimization, we introduce several methodological enhancements. First, we exclude cyclic flow patterns that are physically infeasible, reducing the solution space. Second, we exploit symmetry between supply and return networks to avoid redundant computations. Third, we reduce the number of temperature mixing constraints, which enforce correct temperature propagation at network junctions, by identifying and removing redundant constraints. Additionally, we develop novel primal heuristics to quickly find high-quality feasible solutions, and introduce tailored branching rules to guide the search process more effectively. Each of these ingredients is designed to address specific bottlenecks in solving large, nonlinear, and meshed network instances.\n\nWe evaluate the proposed methods on a diverse set of benchmark instances, including both generated networks with varying sizes, topologies, and numbers of suppliers, as well as real-world networks representative of practical district heating systems. Performance is measured by the number of instances solved to global optimality, total runtime, and the optimality gap achieved. On the generated benchmarks, our methods increase the number of solved instances from 40% to over 85% and reduce median runtime by more than 50% compared to a baseline without these enhancements. For real-world instances, the algorithm consistently finds solutions with optimality gaps below 1% within one hour, demonstrating both solution quality and practical computational efficiency.\n\nFor multi-period operation with thermal storage, we investigate a time decomposition approach that decouples the problem into sequential single-period subproblems linked by storage state variables. Under the assumption that storage charging and discharging decisions can be fixed without loss of optimality\u2014a condition often met in practice for moderate storage sizes and demand profiles\u2014this decomposition yields globally optimal solutions. On a small example network with three nodes and one storage unit, the decomposition approach computes optimal solutions in less than a second, whereas solving the full time-coupled problem directly exceeds one hour. While demonstrated on a small case, the approach is applicable to larger networks under similar assumptions.\n\nIn summary, this work provides new algorithmic tools for the global optimization of district heating networks, clarifies the scope and applicability of the underlying model, and demonstrates substantial computational improvements on both synthetic and real-world benchmarks. These advances contribute to the practical deployment of low-carbon district heating systems by enabling efficient and reliable operational planning."}}